Upserts with reorder_socksql_no_deadlock and reorder_idx_writes on.

An upsert picks between insert, update and doing nothing from the rows it
finds when it is applied, so it must see the transaction's other writes in the
order they were issued. Reordering moved upserts after deletes, and deferring
index deletes made an upsert collide with a key its own transaction had
already deleted, which looked like a verify error and was replayed until the
transaction failed. A transaction that contains an upsert is now applied in
issue order. The noreorder variant runs the same checks with reordering off.
